 cherry-picking  eclecticism you know she morges Borg  approaches they often are very surface  level this is the problem with lifehack  philosophy right it's it doesn't get  deep enough and it doesn't put enough of  those parts that have been cherry picked  back together and that is really what  she's advocating for doing the opposite  of that cherry picking but but cherry  picking the right stuff and then  arranging them in relation to each other  and consolidating it with practice so  you know I like that sort of approach  and I also like the honesty about it  another thing that's really great about  this work are the exercises and here I  want to like I'm gonna  do a little bit of a ramp so I've looked  at and reviewed a lot of recent books  about stoicism also to some degree I  haven't reviewed them so much but I've  looked at a lot on Buddhism as well and  they tend to like circulate the same  basic sets of practices right everyone  unstow assistive includes negative  visualization there's usually like a  paragraph devoted to it and it's usually  not done particularly well and it sounds  like everybody else's version of it so  when you come across a chapter about  practices or as she's calling them  meditations and there's actually quite a  few it contained in other chapters in  the book and they're really well put  together well thought through well  described that's kind of unusual and so  I'm really happy with not only what  she's picked but how she has expressed  it again real expert writing here the  other thing that I do want to say that I  find compelling about this work is she's  doing really excellent comparative  philosophy in here and doing so without  going into you know super great depth  again there's just enough given to the  reader to sort of promote their own  thinking about this and to get them  steered in the right direction so those  are some of the the really high points  of this work for me  now wouldn't be a Sadler's honest review  if I d