hen this follows which seems to be  what she's saying but I don't think that  there's any intrinsic connection between  the two I think you could buy into one  namely the doctrine of the indifference  without buying into the other the  doctrine of Providence which is what  many modern Stoics in fact do the other  thing that I'll say is that I think I  would have liked have seen discussion of  another way of framing more than  happiness so you know the Stoics talk  about eudaimonia and there's a lot of  discussion in here and that's very good  and in the the chapter devoted to more  than happiness right the sort of  linchpin of the book what else is is  happiness well it's you know equanimity  good flow of life all these sorts of  things which mcaro is quite good about  unpacking and going into detail about  but one thing that I didn't see in here  that I think is important for the Stoics  and you see this in cicero's  presentation of kaitos views in on the  ends book 3 and Senecas letters in  Epictetus to a certain degree and in  other places is this notion that what  really matters is is moral goodness and  that's not distinct from happiness if  you if you are aiming at moral goodness  which is encapsulated in virtue and the  four virtues you are going to be happy  but it's it's a kind of happiness that  goes beyond typical you die monastic  views things and so I think I just want  to like to seen a little bit more  clarification of that in there because  it is part of you know traditional  stoicism this is why some people think  there's a really salt you know strong  deontological side to stoicism and they  they almost view the stoic says like in  some respect conscience before can't  is a controversial topic but I thought  it's good to bring up anyway so I don't  have a lot of deep you know serious  complaints or criticisms of the work  those are my only nitpicks  all right final thoughts and assessments  first off let me just say I  unqualifiedly recommend this 